\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The axial degree on a refined grid}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Take the odd-dimensional axial symbol of \ref{fdip-017B}, with #{n=2m+1}, the same ordered Clifford directions and the same spin-fiber on both grids. Carry an integer axial representative #{0<|r_c|<N_c/2} by the \vocabk{site refinement}{fdip-01BP}. On a joint eigenvector of normalized volume sign #{s} and axial Clifford sign #{b}, the increasing transverse product has the same eigenvalue #{(-1)^{n-c}\ui^m s b} on both. In the ordered equal-period transverse plane of \ref{fdip-017C}, with #{n=c=3} and its chosen lifted turn and eighth root, its degree on both grids is #{s b\bmod 8}.}

\proof{\p{The two sine arguments #{2\pi r_c/N_c} and #{2\pi r_c/(kN_c)} both lie strictly between #{-\pi} and #{\pi} with the sign of #{r_c}. Dividing by positive spacings preserves this sign. If #{\xi} is the eigenvalue of the axial Hermitian symbol with coefficient #{f_c}, then #{\operatorname{sgn}(\xi)\operatorname{sgn}(f_c)=b} on either grid. The product formula of \ref{fdip-017B} and the lifted-turn calculation of \ref{fdip-017C} give the assertions. At a coarse even half-period the representative #{+N_c/2} instead has zero centered coefficient on the coarse grid and positive coefficient on the fine grid for #{k>1}.}}
